1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,700
The Singapore Sweep: How
the Lottery Went Legal

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:06,618
On the first Wednesday of every month,

3
00:00:06,698 --> 00:00:12,060
Singapore Pools draws the Singapore
Sweep, a lottery with 3.5 million

4
00:00:12,140 --> 00:00:16,400
seven-digit numbers and a
first prize of $2.3 million.

5
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:20,349
Its rules still describe
it as a "sweepstakes game",

6
00:00:20,429 --> 00:00:24,301
a word that comes from the
racecourse, and the game

7
00:00:24,381 --> 00:00:28,100
itself began at the
Singapore Turf Club in 1966.

8
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:32,190
At the end of 1968 it was
taken over by Singapore Pools,

9
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:37,104
a government-owned company set up
that year to give people a legal

10
00:00:37,184 --> 00:00:41,950
way to bet and to take business
away from the illegal lotteries.

11
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:45,819
Between them the Sweep,
Toto and 4-D became fixtures

12
00:00:45,899 --> 00:00:50,075
of Singapore life, and all
three are still drawn today.

13
00:00:50,075 --> 00:00:53,575
Horse racing came to Singapore early.

14
00:00:53,575 --> 00:00:58,024
The Singapore Sporting Club
was founded in 1842 and held

15
00:00:58,104 --> 00:01:02,875
its races on the course at
Farrer Park, off Serangoon Road.

16
00:01:02,875 --> 00:01:07,602
It became the Singapore
Turf Club in 1924 and moved

17
00:01:07,682 --> 00:01:12,300
to a new racecourse at
Bukit Timah in April 1933.

18
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:17,525
By the 1930s it was running
sweeps alongside its races.

19
00:01:17,525 --> 00:01:21,350
A sweep worked differently
from betting on a horse.

20
00:01:21,350 --> 00:01:25,850
A punter bought a numbered
ticket without choosing anything.

21
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:28,811
Before the race, ticket
numbers were drawn and

22
00:01:28,891 --> 00:01:32,249
each one was matched to a
horse that was due to run,

23
00:01:32,329 --> 00:01:34,775
so most tickets drew no horse at all.

24
00:01:34,775 --> 00:01:39,140
Whoever held the ticket matched to
the winner took the first prize,

25
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:43,200
and smaller prizes went to the
tickets on the placed horses.

26
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:46,144
Knowing about horses gave no advantage,

27
00:01:46,224 --> 00:01:48,550
because nobody picked a horse.

28
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:51,685
It was luck twice over:
first whether a ticket

29
00:01:51,765 --> 00:01:54,700
drew a horse, and then how that horse ran.

30
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:58,268
A ticket that had drawn a
favourite was worth more than

31
00:01:58,348 --> 00:02:01,915
one that had drawn an outsider,
and in the big overseas

32
00:02:01,995 --> 00:02:05,032
sweeps such as the Irish
Hospitals' Sweepstake,

33
00:02:05,112 --> 00:02:09,025
tickets on favourite horses
had a better chance of winning.

34
00:02:09,025 --> 00:02:12,503
The law treated the club's
sweeps as private lotteries

35
00:02:12,583 --> 00:02:16,075
for its members, while public
lotteries were illegal.

36
00:02:16,075 --> 00:02:20,692
In June 1930 a tobacco merchant
named Choo Poh Keng, a member of

37
00:02:20,772 --> 00:02:25,535
the Turf Club, stood trial with
his employee on charges brought by

38
00:02:25,615 --> 00:02:30,452
the police's Gambling Suppression
Department, which accused them of

39
00:02:30,532 --> 00:02:35,375
running a public lottery from his
premises at 35 North Canal Road.

40
00:02:35,375 --> 00:02:39,669
The case turned on whether a member
could give or sell the chances

41
00:02:39,749 --> 00:02:43,925
he had bought in the club's sweep
to his friends and employees.

42
00:02:43,925 --> 00:02:49,016
It was treated as a test case, and in
July the magistrate acquitted Choo,

43
00:02:49,096 --> 00:02:52,000
holding that it was not a public lottery.

44
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,475
The sums could be large.

45
00:02:54,475 --> 00:02:59,027
After the Gold Cup
meeting of September 1933,

46
00:02:59,107 --> 00:03:02,321
The Sunday Times worked out that

47
00:03:02,401 --> 00:03:06,725
$298,750 had gone on the big sweeps alone.

48
00:03:06,725 --> 00:03:11,802
The holder of the ticket that
drew the first prize in the

49
00:03:11,882 --> 00:03:17,400
big sweep on the last day had
paid $5 for it and won $54,340.

50
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:21,250
Racing also raised money
for the war effort.

51
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:25,346
By August 1941 the Singapore Turf Club had

52
00:03:25,426 --> 00:03:30,019
given $266,000 to The
War Fund since June 1940,

53
00:03:30,099 --> 00:03:34,275
and some winners added gifts of their own.

54
00:03:34,275 --> 00:03:38,587
One was Peter Chong, a
bookseller, whose ticket,

55
00:03:38,667 --> 00:03:43,345
number 114291, drew the
first prize in the big sweep

56
00:03:43,425 --> 00:03:48,000
at the Penang races, and
who sent the fund $1,000.

57
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,072
During the Japanese
Occupation the military

58
00:03:51,152 --> 00:03:55,836
administration ran its own government
lottery, the Syonan Syoken,

59
00:03:55,916 --> 00:03:58,775
which I wrote about in an earlier post.

60
00:03:58,775 --> 00:04:03,775
After the war, lotteries were also
run to raise money for causes.

61
00:04:03,775 --> 00:04:08,373
In the early 1950s the
Malayan Chinese Association

62
00:04:08,453 --> 00:04:13,225
ran a series of $1 million
lotteries across Malaya.

63
00:04:13,225 --> 00:04:17,393
In March 1951 The Sunday
Times printed a photograph of

64
00:04:17,473 --> 00:04:22,586
the $250,000 cheque that would go
to the winner of the second one,

65
00:04:22,666 --> 00:04:26,284
to be drawn in Ipoh in
April, and reported that

66
00:04:26,364 --> 00:04:31,712
the association's Singapore branch,
opened only the previous October,

67
00:04:31,792 --> 00:04:35,175
had already sold more
than 120,000 tickets.

68
00:04:35,175 --> 00:04:41,679
In 1953 the government of the Federation
of Malaya banned the association's lottery,

69
00:04:41,759 --> 00:04:45,600
which the association
called "a breach of faith".

70
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:49,300
Beside the legal games ran illegal ones.

71
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:53,405
The best known was chap ji
kee, Hokkien for "12 cards",

72
00:04:53,485 --> 00:04:56,225
a numbers lottery run by syndicates.

73
00:04:56,225 --> 00:05:00,604
Punters bet on a pair of numbers
from 1 to 12, and the syndicates

74
00:05:00,684 --> 00:05:05,075
added up the bets and declared
the least-backed pair the winner.

75
00:05:05,075 --> 00:05:08,660
It was known as the "housewives'
opium", because many of

76
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:12,260
its players were women who
staked a few cents at a time

77
00:05:12,340 --> 00:05:15,875
in the hope of a little extra
money for the household.

78
00:05:15,875 --> 00:05:20,380
The courts were still fining
and jailing people for running

79
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:25,200
it or carrying its betting slips
through the 1950s and 1960s.

80
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:29,372
Lottery tickets from abroad
were sold in Singapore too,

81
00:05:29,452 --> 00:05:32,309
and in June 1967 the government warned

82
00:05:32,389 --> 00:05:36,100
people against buying
tickets for a Malta sweep.

83
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:40,595
In September 1966 the Turf
Club announced that Singapore

84
00:05:40,675 --> 00:05:45,250
would have "its own social
welfare lottery" by December.

85
00:05:45,250 --> 00:05:50,150
It would be called the Singapore
Sweep and run entirely by the club.

86
00:05:50,150 --> 00:05:54,920
It would carry more than 2,000
prizes totalling $1 million,

87
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,784
with a first prize of
$350,000, and its profits

88
00:05:58,864 --> 00:06:02,975
would go to sports development
and social welfare.

89
00:06:02,975 --> 00:06:06,290
Tickets cost $1, and the plan was to hold

90
00:06:06,370 --> 00:06:09,600
a draw at every Singapore race meeting.

91
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:14,504
By the end of October more than
two million tickets were on sale,

92
00:06:14,584 --> 00:06:19,875
and the first Singapore Sweep was
drawn on the 12th of December 1966.

93
00:06:19,875 --> 00:06:24,448
Voluntary organisations worried
that the new lottery would compete

94
00:06:24,528 --> 00:06:29,454
with their own fundraising, and in
April 1967 the Minister for Finance,

95
00:06:29,534 --> 00:06:34,037
Lim Kim San, assured the Singapore
Council of Social Service that

96
00:06:34,117 --> 00:06:38,700
it would not take away the means
they already had to raise funds.

97
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:42,250
The club also ran a four-digit lottery.

98
00:06:42,250 --> 00:06:45,815
There had been a rush for
the tickets at its office

99
00:06:45,895 --> 00:06:50,104
in Robinson Road, and in May
1966, when the queues had gone,

100
00:06:50,184 --> 00:06:53,400
the club reported that
sales were still high.

101
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:58,403
Its winning numbers were taken from
the sweeps: the 4-D pool for the

102
00:06:58,483 --> 00:07:02,813
27th of November 1966, for
example, was based on the first,

103
00:07:02,893 --> 00:07:08,275
second and third prizes and the
starters of the Perak Turf Club's sweep.

104
00:07:08,275 --> 00:07:11,847
In April 1968 the
government announced that it

105
00:07:11,927 --> 00:07:15,500
would introduce pool
betting later that year.

106
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:20,415
The Eastern Sun reported two of
its aims: to save foreign exchange,

107
00:07:20,495 --> 00:07:23,925
and to beat the illegal
chap ji kee operators.

108
00:07:23,925 --> 00:07:29,535
The new game, called Toto, was being
introduced to Singapore by the Bulgarians,

109
00:07:29,615 --> 00:07:33,000
and it was based on a
Bulgarian numbers system.

110
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,455
Singapore Pools (Private) Limited was

111
00:07:36,535 --> 00:07:41,025
incorporated on the 23rd
of May 1968 to run it.

112
00:07:41,025 --> 00:07:44,472
Toto went on sale at
the end of May through

113
00:07:44,552 --> 00:07:47,750
about 500 agents and every post office.

114
00:07:47,750 --> 00:07:53,174
Players crossed off six numbers
out of 49 for a stake of 50 cents,

115
00:07:53,254 --> 00:07:58,425
and the advertisements promised
a top prize of up to $500,000.

116
00:07:58,425 --> 00:08:02,479
The first draw was held
on Sunday the 9th of June

117
00:08:02,559 --> 00:08:06,614
1968 at the Victoria
Theatre, the same hall where

118
00:08:06,694 --> 00:08:10,575
the Occupation lottery
had been drawn in 1942.

119
00:08:10,575 --> 00:08:14,345
It was shown on television,
and seven of Singapore's

120
00:08:14,425 --> 00:08:18,275
top sportsmen and sportswomen
took part in the draw.

121
00:08:18,275 --> 00:08:22,575
Nearly $280,000 had been staked.

122
00:08:22,575 --> 00:08:25,769
Nobody matched all six numbers,

123
00:08:25,849 --> 00:08:31,025
and two people shared the
third prize of $27,840.

124
00:08:31,025 --> 00:08:34,881
At the end of 1968 Singapore
Pools took over the

125
00:08:34,961 --> 00:08:39,225
Sweep as well, with its
sale, distribution and draw.

126
00:08:39,225 --> 00:08:43,581
The Parliamentary Secretary
for Finance, Tang See Chim,

127
00:08:43,661 --> 00:08:48,500
told Parliament that the first
prize would rise to $400,000.

128
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:52,737
The first Sweep run by
Singapore Pools was drawn at

129
00:08:52,817 --> 00:08:57,050
the National Theatre on
the 28th of February 1969.

130
00:08:57,050 --> 00:09:02,284
Later draws moved around the
city's halls and amusement parks:

131
00:09:02,364 --> 00:09:07,941
the Gay World Stadium in May 1969,
the Kreta Ayer People's Theatre

132
00:09:08,021 --> 00:09:13,250
in July 1969, and the Wonderland
amusement park in July 1970.

133
00:09:13,250 --> 00:09:17,009
Raising money to build a
National Stadium was one of

134
00:09:17,089 --> 00:09:19,962
the driving forces behind legal betting,

135
00:09:20,042 --> 00:09:24,908
and proceeds from the Sweep and
Toto paid a substantial part of the

136
00:09:24,988 --> 00:09:28,975
bill for the stadium at
Kallang, which opened in 1973.

137
00:09:28,975 --> 00:09:33,450
The Turf Club kept its 4-D
game for another two decades.

138
00:09:33,450 --> 00:09:38,342
In May 1986 Singapore Pools
began selling 4-D tickets through

139
00:09:38,422 --> 00:09:43,478
computer terminals at 97 of its
outlets, and within three weeks

140
00:09:43,558 --> 00:09:48,775
the Turf Club reported that sales
at its own outlets had fallen.

141
00:09:48,775 --> 00:09:52,152
That June, Singapore
Pools also said it would

142
00:09:52,232 --> 00:09:55,838
carry out spot-checks to
make sure that Toto and

143
00:09:55,918 --> 00:09:59,375
4-D tickets were not
sold to anyone under 18.

144
00:09:59,375 --> 00:10:04,628
In 2004 the Singapore Totalisator
Board, known as the Tote Board,

145
00:10:04,708 --> 00:10:08,400
bought Singapore Pools
from Temasek Holdings.

146
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:12,339
Under the new arrangement
Singapore Pools ran 4-D, Toto,

147
00:10:12,419 --> 00:10:16,143
the Sweep and football
betting on the board's behalf,

148
00:10:16,223 --> 00:10:21,175
and the Turf Club stopped running
4-D to concentrate on horse racing.

149
00:10:21,175 --> 00:10:24,376
Singapore Pools is still the only operator

150
00:10:24,456 --> 00:10:27,425
allowed to run lotteries in Singapore.

151
00:10:27,425 --> 00:10:31,398
It is owned by the Tote Board,
and its surplus goes to the

152
00:10:31,478 --> 00:10:35,950
board to fund charities, sport,
the arts and community projects.

153
00:10:35,950 --> 00:10:39,027
In 2016 the Ministry of Home Affairs

154
00:10:39,107 --> 00:10:41,825
allowed it to take bets online.

155
00:10:41,825 --> 00:10:46,798
Its games are now regulated by
the Gambling Regulatory Authority,

156
00:10:46,878 --> 00:10:51,775
which approves their rules under
the Gambling Control Act 2022.

157
00:10:51,775 --> 00:10:55,650
Horse racing, where the
sweeps began, has ended.

158
00:10:55,650 --> 00:10:59,369
The Turf Club held its
last race meeting at Kranji

159
00:10:59,449 --> 00:11:03,092
on the 5th of October
2024, and the land is being

160
00:11:03,172 --> 00:11:07,275
returned to the government
for housing and other uses.

161
00:11:07,275 --> 00:11:10,775
The Sweep has carried
on without the horses.

162
00:11:10,775 --> 00:11:14,742
Its tickets carry seven-digit numbers from

163
00:11:14,822 --> 00:11:19,270
1000000 to 4499999, and
each draw picks out the

164
00:11:19,350 --> 00:11:23,413
winning numbers for
the first prize of $2.3

165
00:11:23,493 --> 00:11:27,925
million and the smaller
prizes down to $1,000.

166
00:11:27,925 --> 00:11:32,650
A separate draw of two-digit
endings pays $6 each.

167
00:11:32,650 --> 00:11:34,943
Where it fits in the bigger story:

168
00:11:35,023 --> 00:11:40,736
Singapore's legal lotteries grew out of
the race clubs and charitable associations,

169
00:11:40,816 --> 00:11:44,375
at a time when the illegal
ones ran alongside them.

170
00:11:44,375 --> 00:11:49,684
In 1968 the government brought the games
under a single state-owned company,

171
00:11:49,764 --> 00:11:51,882
with the stated aims of drawing

172
00:11:51,962 --> 00:11:56,491
money away from illegal syndicates
and keeping it in the country,

173
00:11:56,571 --> 00:11:59,975
and the surplus has since
gone to public causes.

174
00:11:59,975 --> 00:12:03,904
The racecourse that gave the
Sweep its name has now closed,

175
00:12:03,984 --> 00:12:07,369
but the word "sweepstakes"
survives in the rules of

176
00:12:07,449 --> 00:12:11,050
a game that no longer has
anything to do with horses.
